          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Denis Diderot</strong><br>\nDenis Diderot was born on October 5, 1713, in Langres, France. He was the son of a cutlery maker and received a classical education before moving to Paris. In Paris, he initially studied law, but his passion for literature and philosophy soon took precedence. Despite his father's wishes for him to pursue a career in law, Diderot decided to become a writer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Diderot's Literary Career</strong><br>\nDiderot began his career as a translator, but he quickly started to write original works. His early philosophical works were not well received, and he struggled financially. However, he persevered and started to publish plays and novels, which displayed his wit and intellectual prowess. His novel \"La Religieuse\" (The Nun) was written during this period, showcasing his skepticism towards institutional religion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Encyclopedia Project</strong><br>\nIn 1745, Diderot took on the monumental task of editing the \"Encyclopédie.\" This was a massive scholarly work that aimed to compile and disseminate the sum of the world's knowledge. The project took over two decades to complete and included contributions from some of the greatest minds of the Enlightenment.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
