          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Diderot and the Enlightenment</strong><br>\nAs an editor and writer for the \"Encyclopédie,\" Diderot became a prominent figure in the Enlightenment. His work on the encyclopedia and his writings promoted critical thinking and secular values. Diderot believed in the power of education and knowledge to improve society and individual lives.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Controversies</strong><br>\nDiderot's encyclopedia faced opposition from the French government and the Catholic Church. The project was condemned for its secular and critical stance on traditional authority. Despite the censorship and the threat of imprisonment, Diderot continued to work on the \"Encyclopédie,\" often in secrecy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Diderot's Philosophical Views</strong><br>\nDiderot was a materialist, meaning he believed that only material or physical things truly exist. He argued that thought and consciousness arose from the material interactions within the brain. His writings often explored the relationship between the individual, society, and morality from this perspective.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
