          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to Art and Aesthetics</strong><br>\nApart from philosophy and literature, Diderot also contributed to the field of art criticism. He wrote \"The Salon,\" a series of art reviews, which offered a new perspective on art criticism. His reviews helped establish the genre as an important form of literature in its own right.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Death</strong><br>\nTowards the end of his life, Diderot's intellectual contributions were increasingly recognized. He was visited by other prominent thinkers of the time and corresponded with them on various subjects. Denis Diderot died on July 31, 1784, in Paris, leaving behind a legacy as a key figure of the French Enlightenment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Diderot's Legacy</strong><br>\nDiderot's legacy is primarily as a thinker who challenged existing norms and as a pivotal figure in the spread of Enlightenment ideas. The \"Encyclopédie\" remains his greatest achievement, influencing future generations. His ideas paved the way for the critical, scientific, and secular thinking that characterized the modern era.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
